<!-- @before Staff Engineer Thinking :: staff -->
### Keep the safety conditions in the summary

Raft’s majority-counting commit rule applies directly to current-term entries. Prior-term entries become committed indirectly through a current-term commit; an uncommitted entry can survive or be overwritten. Eligible logs and durable voting rules preserve committed entries across terms. A linearizable read also needs the protocol’s read safety steps. [Raft §§5.4 and 8](https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf).

<!-- @before Answer Key :: guide -->
### Read the short answers with the protocol rules

In answer 6, “not committed” does not mean “certainly lost”: the entry may survive a later election. In answer 7, a new leader need not be a particular old-majority member; the vote/log eligibility rules ensure it contains committed entries. For answer 3, a reachable quorum enables the protocol, while linearizable reads still require its read-safety procedure. [Raft §§5.4 and 8](https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf).
